    {
      "recommendationid": "224638459",
      "author": {
        "steamid": "76561198054996004",
        "personaname": "Mister T ypo",
        "persona_status": "offline",
        "profile_url": "https://steamcommunity.com/id/gnommishmeerkat/",
        "num_games_owned": 0,
        "num_reviews": 9,
        "playtime_forever": 9069,
        "playtime_last_two_weeks": 1882,
        "playtime_at_review": 8953,
        "last_played": 1777781293,
        "avatar": "09ad2d9cba1cf8adea8692f486f2c7a83ad39b52"
      },
      "language": "english",
      "review": "Have you ever looked up at the night sky's stars, and wanted to travel between them?\r\n\r\nEver wanted to play a game that feels like it has a human soul, in the most endearing way?\r\n\r\nNo Man's Sky is either Space Minecraft or a completely transcendent experience.\r\nYou can have the simplest, most straightforward time you could ask for just planet hopping or ship building.\r\nOr go as extreme as managing fleets and settlements, talking to Literally God, slaying hundreds of foes at your leasure while scrapping crashed starships, raiding crashed and running freighters, smuggle contraband and discover some of the most amazing planets and sights you could ask for.\r\n\r\nEven 10 years after inception there is some bugs and glitches here and there - but if you know this game, you know of its beginnings, and honestly the experience not being completely polished gives that... something else, like this game has apologized, grown, and continues to make itself something better.\r\nJust so we can explore its universe, quite literally, Better. Faster. In our own custom starships. With our friends, or in... a space pickup truck I guess\r\n\r\nNo Man's Sky has been through and changed so much in its ten years that it almost feels like it has human qualities, and it only adds to the addictive gameplay.\r\n\r\nAnd Sean Murray, if you ever read this, I hope you forgave yourself, company, everything. You earned it man. You didn't give us the whole world, or galaxy. You gave us an infinite number of them, brother.\r\n",
